index 70f14d0..066c65a 100644
--- a/docs/sorting.md
+++ b/docs/sorting.md
@@ -39,6 +39,8 @@ With `sortMode: "gpu"` (WebGPU's default), each render runs, after the projectio
 
 Nothing is read back, so each frame is drawn in order for the current camera, with any number of cameras. `WgpuSplatSorter` combines the three ([wgpu.md](wgpu.md#wgpusplatsorter)); `splatRenderer.readSortedCount()` reads the visible count back for stats.
 
+When the application knows its peak input size, call `splatRenderer.reserveSortCapacity(count)` during loading to allocate the GPU sort's growing buffers up front. Use the number of input splats before visibility culling, such as `collection.count` or the planned peak streaming selection. The call has no effect in CPU sort mode or on the WebGL2 backend. Larger inputs can still grow the buffers, and repeating the call within the existing capacity reuses them. The renderer holds the allocation until disposal. At the exact requested capacity, the variable-size GPU buffers use about 16.5 bytes per input splat, with corresponding CPU-side arrays. Reservation has no effect on projected splat textures or first-use shader and pipeline creation.
+
 Sorting more keys than the device's storage binding limit allows (by default 256 MiB, or 64M keys) throws an error naming the limit; `createRenderer({ limits: { maxStorageBufferBindingSize: "adapter" } })` asks for all the adapter has ([renderer.md](renderer.md#device-baselines)).
 
